\title{Bartlett adjustment for random effects meta-analysis of event outcomes with small samples}
% \date{}
\author[1]{Haben Michael}
\affil[1]{University of Massachusetts, Amherst, MA (hmichael@math.umass.edu)}
\date{}                   
\begin{document}
\maketitle

Abstract. ((stijnen)) describes 4 random effects models for
meta-analyses of involving event outcomes. Inference for these models
is based on an asymptotic chi-squared approximation. We describe a
procedure for Bartlett correction of the confidence interval,
improving the convergence rate from $1/\n$ to $1/\n^2$ under ideal
conditions. We apply the proposed procedure to the meta-analysis
considered by ((stijnen 2010)), finding a differences in CI lengths up to
10\%.

\textsc{Keywords: Meta-analysis, Bartlett correction,
  Generalized linear mixed models}


\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

There is currently underway a move to favor generalized linear mixed models in
carrying out meta-analyses of event outcomes, particularly 
when sample sizes are small or events are rare. GLMMs
avoid many of the dubious assumptions, such as gaussianity of the effects and
perfect knowledge of standard deviations, required by the traditional alternative,
inverse-variance weighting. % have been since stijnen
% 2010, glmm have become the preferred model for random effects
% meta-analysis based on count data. cochran etc have recommended ... .
However, inferences under GLMMs are still based on an asymptotic
chi-squared approximation. Therefore, as in the case of
inverse-variance weighted estimation, the conclusions of smaller
meta-analyses are suspect, though smaller  meta-analyses are where the use
of GLMMs is often recommended. A classical remedy is Bartlett
correction, which leverages modelling assumptions to improve the
efficiency of the testing procedure. When the modelling assumptions
are met, the improvement is typically a jump in the rate of asymptotic
convergence from $1/\n$ to $1/\n^2$. [[the key modelling assumption in
this setting is the gaussianity of the random effect.]]

While Bartlett corrections have long been applied to generalized
linear models [[cite]], there do not appear to be extensions to even
the simple GLMMs that are used in meta-analysis. The reason for the
reluctance appears to be computational difficulty [[cite
hanada]]. While computer algebra systems have long been applied to
compute the third- and fourth-order derivative arrays needed for Bartlett correction of GLMs ((cite
old cordeiro paper, andrews and stafford, mccullagh maybe)), closed-form expressions for GLMM Bartlett corrections are unavailable for any of the commonly used
models other than the normal-normal/identity link ((cite noma,
michael2026)). A combination of automatic symbolic manipulation and
numerical approximation appears
necessary. % Unlike in the case of GLMs, closed-form expressions are
% unavailable for Bartlett corrections for GLMMs.[[check there are closed forms for glms!! ]]
We argue that with
careful design these difficulties can be overcome and consumer-grade
hardware is sufficient for practical implementation.

In the next section we summarize four event data meta-analysis models,
following [[Stifjnen's]] exposition. We then describe the method of
Bartlett correction in broad terms, and discuss some of the underlying
assumptions. In Section \ref{sec:method} we offer a practical approach
to estimation of the Bartlett correction applicfable to the four
models described in Section \ref{sec:background}. In Section
\ref{sec:simulation} we examine the effectiveness and robustness of
the corrected test, finding .... In Section \ref{sec:data-analysis} we
re-visit the data analyses of ((stijnen)). We find that, though the
differences in CIs may be negligible for moderate or large
meta-analyses, for smaller meta-analyses the difference may be clinically relevant.


