lawley ((cite)) provides a formula for the correction ((ref)) as sums
and products of the components of the inverse Fisher information for
((ref likelihood)) and certain tensors [[k terms]]. Being a small set
of sums and products this formula does not introduce numerical or
computational difficulties that aren't already present in the basic
tensor components input into the formula, and we focus here on these  components. To this end, let $D_r$ denote partial differentiation with respect to a
sequence of the parameters $\effect$ and $\tau^2$ given by $r$. For
example, $D_{\mu,\tau^2}\sum_{i=1}^n\log L_i(\effect,\tau^2\mid \Y_i)$
denotes the off-diagonal term of the observed information matrix for
((ref total likelihood)). Define $\k_r=\k_r^{(0)}=\E D_r \sum_{i=1}^n\log L_i(\effect,\tau^2\mid \Y_i)$ and
$\k_r^{(s)} = D_s \k_r$. In this notation, for example, the Fisher information
matrix has components
$\k_{\mu,\mu}, \k_{\mu,\tau^2}, \k_{\tau^2,\tau^2}$. Lawley's formula
for the Bartlett correction requires all terms $\k_r$ where $r$
is of length 2, 3, or 4, and all terms $\k_r^{(s)}$ where $r$ is of
length $3$ and $s$ of length $1$ or $r$ is of length $2$ and $s$ of
length $1$ or $2$. Taking into account permutation symmetry of the
order of differentiation, there are $35$ such terms in a model with one parameter of interest and one nuisance paramter.

By linearity of expectation and differentiation, it suffices to
compute the terms $\k_r^{(s)}$ for the unit log-likelihoods
$\log L_i(\effect,\tau^2\mid \Y_i), i=1,\ldots,\n$.\comment{Since
  meta-analysis data is typically non-identically distributed, the
  tensor components ((k)) must be computed separately for each study.}
Let us consider a single study from the meta-analysis, dropping the
subscript $i$ from the conditional PMFs and likelihoods. Then the general tensor component needed for the computation of Bartlett's correction is
\begin{align}
  k_r^{(s)} 
  &=  \sum_y D_s\left( \left(D_r \log\int f(y|\z)\phi(\z)d\z\right)  \int \w_k f(y|\z)\phi(\z)d\z\right)  
\end{align}
The outer sum is taken over the support of $\Y$.

