\subsection{Differentiation}

Carrying the differentiations ((ref above display)) through yields
rational expressions in derivatives of the weighted sums
$\sum_{j} \w_j f(\y|\z_j)$, say,
$\F_{kl}(\y)=\sum_{j} \w_j \frac{d^k}{d\mu^k}\frac{d^l}{d(\tau^2)^l}
f(y|\z_j)$. These expressions for ((k)) in terms of the $\F_{kl}$ are
the same for any model based on a likelihood of the form ((ref))  and may be pre-computed. For example
$\k_{\mu,\mu}=
\sum_\y(\F_{20}(\y)-(\F_{10}(\y)^2/\F_{00}(\y))$), % and $\k_{\mu,\tau^2}^{(\tau^2)}=(\F_{01}^2\F_{10})/\F_{00}^2 - (\F_{02}\F_{10} + \F_{01}\F_{11})/
% \F_{00} + \F_{12}$,
and $\k_{\mu,\tau^2}^{(\tau^2)}=\sum_\y(\F_{01}(\y)^2\F_{10}(\y)/\F_{00}(\y)^2 - (\F_{02}(\y)\F_{10}(\y) + \F_{01}(\y)\F_{11}(\y))/
\F_{00}(\y) + \F_{12}(\y))$.

The derivatives $\F_{jk}(\y)$ of the model-specific conditional PMFs
that appear in the 35 tensor components number 15: $\F_{jk}(\y)$ for
$0\le j+k \le 4$. The form of these derivatives may be derived
independently of data but their specific values in general depend on
parameters of the conditional PMF ((see above)), and must typically be
computed for each study of a meta-analysis. The latter computations
may be carried out in parallel when the meta-analysis consists of
stochatically independent studies, such as we consider
here. Furthermore, since the conditional PMFs only depend on the model
parameters $(\effect,\tau^2)$ through the PMF's distributional
parameters, the derivatives $\F_{jk}(\y), 0\le j+k \le 4$, may be
re-written as linear combinations [[linear for binomial and hyperg
also??]] of $\F_{00}(\y)=\sum_{j} \w_j f(y|\theta_j)$ using standard
recurrences ((cite johnson and kotz univariate discrete
distributions)). The advantage of this form is that the terms
$\F_{00}$ involve only the PMF itself, for which optimized and
numerically stable routines are available. For example, two
applications of the Poisson PMF recurrence
$ \frac{d}{d\lambda} f_{\lambda}(\y\mid \Z) = f_{\lambda}(\y-1\mid \Z)
- f_{\lambda}(\y\mid \Z), \y\ge 1, $ yield, for model ((ref
poisson/incidence rate model)),
\begin{align}
  \F_{11}(\y) &= \sum_j\w_j \frac{d^2}{d\mu d\tau^2} f_{\lambda(\effect,\tau^2)}(\y\mid \z_j)\\
              &= \frac{d\lambda}{d\mu} \frac{d\lambda}{d\tau^2}\F_{00}(\y-2)
                +( \frac{d^2\lambda}{d\mu d\tau^2} - 2 \frac{d\lambda}{d\mu} \frac{d\lambda}{d\tau^2}) \F_{00}(\y-1)
                + \frac{d\lambda}{d\mu} \frac{d\lambda}{d\tau^2} \F_{00}(\y),\qquad \y\ge 2
\end{align}

In summary. the approach is, for each of the $\n$ studies in the
meta-analysis: for each $\y$ in the effective support of study $i$
evaluate the 15 terms $\F_{jk}(\y)$ [[these are vectors across y actually]] using only routines for the
conditional PMF (Poisson, binomial, or hypergeometric) and basic
arithmetic, and then compute the ((35)) k terms using only the
computed 15 ((F)) terms and basic arithmetic. The 15 $\F_{jk}(\y)$
terms can be computed in parallel, and once they are computed the 35
((k)) terms can be computed in parallel. As the studies are
independent the entire computation of the study-level Bartlett correction can be parallelized across the $\n$ studies, as well.


