\section{Simulation}
\label{sec:simulation}

we examine by simulation the rate at which the LR and
Bartlett-corrected LR statistics converge to the reference chi-squared
distribution under the four models ((ref)). The purpose is three-fold:
1. to investigate whether the quadrature approximations used by the
proposed method are sufficient, 2. to investigate whether the bartlett
correction is effective as applied to this type of lattice data, as
there are few theoretical results here, and 3. to assess the
robustness the of any improvement in the rate offered by the corrected
statistic to deviations from the assumed non-degenerate Gaussian model
for the random effect.

\subsection{Design}
To make the simulation results relevant, the simulated meta-analyses
are constructed to resemble the data of the stijnen tables ((data
analysis)). The true effect and heterogeneity $\munull$ and
$\tausqnull$ are taken to be the MLE's for the data obtained by
((stijnen et al.)), and the distributional parameters are obtained by
sampling with replacement from the ((stijnen tables)). For example,
for the control-arm ((bn-prop)) model, a simulated meta-analysis of
size $\n$ is drawn by 1. sampling with replacement $\n$ trial sizes
$\C{\m}$ from the $18$ control arm study sizes in ((ref nw18 data
set)), 2. sampling $\n$ effects $\Y$ under the binomial likelihood
((ref)) using the sampled trial sizes and the parameters
$(\munull,\tausqnull)=(...)$ taken from the MLE calculations of
((stijnen2010)). Meta-analyses of sizes $5,\ldots,15,$ are sampled in
this manner, and the rate of convergence of the empirical
Kolmogorov-Smirnov distance to a chi-squared distribution is
estimated. ((see other ma paper for details on rate estimation)).

Robustness of the gaussianity assumption is examined by perturbing a
normal to a skewed distribution ((cite pusey-jones paper)). the skew
was chosen since it drives the first-order error in CLT-type
approximations. Robustness against non-degeneracy of the random effect, $\tausqnull>0$, is studied in particular cases below.%  the sinh-asinch model was used to obtain the
% perturbned gaussian distributions.

In all simulations,  we have used the true parameters
$(\munull,\tausqnull)$ to calculate the Bartlett correction
$b=b(\munull,\tausqnull)$ [[perturbed by a Gaussian with SD
$1/\sqrt\n$??]]. In practice the MLE would be used. The practical
reason is to avoid the prohibitive computational cost of
re-calculating the Bartlett calculation for each of the $\n^4$ monte
carlo replicates. The justification is the result of [[barndorff and
cox]] that plugging in $\sqrt\n-$consistent estimators into the
Bartlett correction term $b(\mu,\tau^2)$ only introduces an
$O(1/\n^2)$ error.


